<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<handout component="day22" xml:id="day-22-lecture">
  <title>Day 22</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      8.1: Decision Trees
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <objectives>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          Understand how a decision tree makes decisions.
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                Given a tree and a data point with multiple predictors
                <m>(x_1,x_2,\ldots,x_p)</m>, derive the predicted outcome
                <m>\hat{y}</m>.
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                Do this for both regression and classification decision trees.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Identify the different parts of a decision tree and explain what they
          represent.
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                Identify leaves (terminal nodes), internal nodes, and branches
                (edges).
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                Given a simple example (e.g., two predictors), identify which
                regions of the predictor space map to each leaf.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Understand how to fit a decision tree.
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                Identify the cost function for regression and classification
                trees.
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                Explain how recursive binary splitting works.
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                Explain when and how the splitting process ends.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Identify the meta-parameters of decision trees that determine their
          flexibility.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Explain why decision trees need to be pruned.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Compare the advantages and disadvantages of decision trees and linear
          regression.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <paragraphs>
    <title>Big idea</title>

    <sidebyside widths="45% 45%">
      <stack>
        <p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                Previously, we've always had some form of function for
                prediction:
                <md>
                  f(X_1,\ldots,X_p)=\hat{Y}
                </md>.
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                Now, we make a sequence of decisions to make a prediction,
                either for regression or classification.
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                Recall: regression means the outcome is quantitative, while
                classification means the outcome is qualitative.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </stack>

      <stack>
        <image source="figures/binarytree-faces-w800.png">
          <shortdescription>
            A binary decision tree using modal verbs Should, Could, Would, and
            Did leading to various emotional faces.
          </shortdescription>
        </image>

        <p>
          Image:
          <url href="https://marekbennett.com/2014/02/14/decision-tree/">Decision tree illustration</url>.
        </p>
      </stack>
    </sidebyside>
  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs>
    <title>Subset of Hitters data</title>

    <sidebyside widths="35% 45%">
      <image source="figures/Hitters-TreeSlides.png">
        <shortdescription>
          Table displaying baseball hitter data with columns for Hits, Years,
          Salary, and LogSalary.
        </shortdescription>
      </image>

      <stack>
        <p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                Remove observations with missing salary values.
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                Log-transform salary to obtain a distribution that is closer to
                a bell shape.
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                Goal: predict log salary.
                We can reverse the transformation by returning <m>\exp(x)</m> if
                the model returns <m>x</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </stack>
    </sidebyside>
  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs>
    <title>First decision tree example</title>

    <sidebyside widths="25% 45%">
      <image source="figures/Hitters-TreeSlides-OnlyLog.png">
        <shortdescription>
          Table displaying baseball hitter data with columns for Hits, Years,
          and LogSalary.
        </shortdescription>
      </image>

      <image source="figures/Fig8-1.png">
        <shortdescription>
          Regression tree for baseball player salaries splitting on Years and
          Hits to produce three leaf nodes.
        </shortdescription>
      </image>
    </sidebyside>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Top split assigns observations with <m>Years &lt; 4.5</m> (in major
            leagues) to the left branch.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Return the mean response for players in that group.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Predictions:
          </p>

          <p>
            <ul>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Mean log salary is 5.107, so the predicted salary is
                  <m>\exp(5.107)=\$165,174</m> thousand dollars.
                </p>
              </li>

              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>5.999 \Rightarrow \$402,834</m>.
                </p>
              </li>

              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>6.740 \Rightarrow \$845,346</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs>
    <title>Interpretation of example</title>

    <sidebyside widths="35% 45%">
      <image source="figures/Fig8-1.png">
        <shortdescription>
          Regression tree for baseball player salaries splitting on Years and
          Hits to produce three leaf nodes.
        </shortdescription>
      </image>

      <stack>
        <p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                Years is the most important factor for determining salary.
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                Players with less experience earn lower salaries than more
                experienced players.
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                For less experienced players, the number of hits plays little
                role in salary.
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                For more experienced players, the number of hits affects salary.
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                This is likely an oversimplification of the real relationship,
                but it is easier to interpret and has a nice graphical
                representation.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </stack>
    </sidebyside>
  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs>
    <title>Regions defined by the tree</title>

    <sidebyside widths="35% 45%">
      <image source="figures/Fig8-1.png">
        <shortdescription>
          Regression tree for baseball player salaries splitting on Years and
          Hits to produce three leaf nodes.
        </shortdescription>
      </image>

      <stack>
        <p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                Note that the tree is is drawn <q>upside-down</q>
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                Leaves (terminal nodes) of the tree:
              </p>

              <p>
                <ul>
                  <li>
                    <p>
                      <m>R_1=\{X\mid Years&lt;4.5\}</m>
                    </p>
                  </li>

                  <li>
                    <p>
                      <m>R_2=\{X\mid Years\geq 4.5,\ Hits&lt;117.5\}</m>
                    </p>
                  </li>

                  <li>
                    <p>
                      <m>R_3=\{X\mid Years\geq 4.5,\ Hits\geq 117.5\}</m>
                    </p>
                  </li>
                </ul>
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                Other splits are called internal nodes.
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                Segments that connect nodes are called branches or edges.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </stack>
    </sidebyside>
  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs>
    <title>Viewing Regions Defined by Tree</title>

    <sidebyside widths="35% 45%">
      <image source="figures/Fig8-1.png">
        <shortdescription>
          Regression tree for baseball player salaries splitting on Years and
          Hits to produce three leaf nodes.
        </shortdescription>
      </image>

      <image source="figures/Fig8-2.png">
        <shortdescription>
          Scatter plot of Hits versus Years partitioned into three regions R1,
          R2, and R3 by a regression tree.
        </shortdescription>
      </image>
    </sidebyside>
  </paragraphs>

  <example>
    <title>Expressing the regions of a regression tree</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Given the following two splits, answer the following questions.
        express the shaded region <m>R</m> using set notation <m>R=\{X|...\}</m>
        and sketch the corresponding tree.
      </p>

      <image source="figures/decision_tree_notation_ex1.png" width="70%">
        <shortdescription>
          Two squares, a and b, showing a square grid from zero to 1 with X1 on
          the x-axis and X2 on the Yaxis.
          The grid splits the square into four equi-spaces regions.
          One square is shaded in each figure at the following location.
        </shortdescription>
      </image>
    </introduction>

    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Sketch the corresponding tree.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <answer>
        <image source="figures/decision_tree_notation_ex1a_answer.png" width="70%">
          <shortdescription>
            The decision tree for the shaded region R.
          </shortdescription>
        </image>
      </answer>
    </task>

    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Express the shaded region <m>R</m> using set notation
          <m>R=\{X|...\}</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <answer>
        <p>
          <m>R=\{X\mid X_1 \geq 0.5, X_2&lt;0.5, X_1&lt;0.75, X_2\geq 0.25\}</m>
        </p>
      </answer>
    </task>
  </example>

  <paragraphs>
    <title>How do we actually get the tree? Two steps</title>

    <sidebyside widths="45% 45%">
      <stack>
        <p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <p>
                We divide the predictor space -- that is, the set of possible
                values for <m>X_1, X_2, \ldots, X_p</m> -- into <m>J</m>
                distinct and non-overlapping regions,
                <m>R_1, R_2, \ldots, R_J</m>.
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                For every observation that falls into the region <m>R_j</m>, we
                make the same prediction: the mean of the response values for
                the training observations in <m>R_j</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </stack>

      <image source="figures/Fig8-3b.png">
        <shortdescription>
          A two-dimensional feature space partitioned into five rectangular
          regions R1 through R5 by vertical and horizontal splits.
        </shortdescription>
      </image>
    </sidebyside>
  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs>
    <title>Step 1: How do we decide on <m>R_j</m>s?</title>

    <sidebyside widths="45% 45%">
      <stack>
        <p>
          Goal:
        </p>

        <p>
          Find boxes <m>R_1,\ldots,R_J</m> that minimize
        </p>

        <p>
          <md>
            \sum_{j=1}^J \sum_{i \in R_j} (y_i-\hat y_{R_j})^2
          </md>
        </p>

        <p>
          <m>\hat y_{R_j}</m> = mean response for training observations in the
          <m>j</m>th box.
        </p>
      </stack>

      <stack>
        <p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                Cost is just RSS.
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                Can't actually check every possible partition.
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                Instead, use a top-down greedy approach called
                <m>\text{Recursive binary splitting}</m>.
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                Begins at the top of the tree with all data points.
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                Uses the best split at every step.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </stack>
    </sidebyside>
  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs>
    <title>Recursive Binary Splitting</title>

    <sidebyside widths="45% 45%">
      <stack>
        <p>
          One split:
        </p>

        <p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                Pick <m>X_j</m> and cutpoint <m>s</m>.
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                Choose them so that splitting into <m>\{X \mid X_j &lt; s\}</m>
                and <m>\{X \mid X_j \geq s\}</m> results in the largest possible
                reduction in RSS.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>

        <p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                Note here we look at all combinations of <m>X_j</m> and <m>s</m>
                for each split of the tree.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </stack>

      <stack>
        <p>
          <md>
            \begin{aligned}
          R_1(j,s) &amp;= \{X \mid X_j &lt; s\}\\
          R_2(j,s) &amp;= \{X \mid X_j \geq s\}
          \end{aligned}
          </md>
        </p>

        <p>
          <md>
            \begin{aligned}
          &amp;\sum_{i \mid x_i \in R_1(j,s)}(y_i - \hat y_{R_1})^2\\
          &amp;+\sum_{i \mid x_i \in R_2(j,s)}(y_i - \hat y_{R_2})^2
          \end{aligned}
          </md>
        </p>
      </stack>
    </sidebyside>
  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs>
    <title>Rinse and repeat</title>

    <sidebyside widths="45% 45%">
      <image source="figures/Fig8-3c.png">
        <shortdescription>
          Decision tree diagram illustrating the sequence of binary splits on X1
          and X2 resulting in regions R1 through R5.
        </shortdescription>
      </image>

      <image source="figures/Fig8-3b.png">
        <shortdescription>
          A two-dimensional feature space partitioned into five rectangular
          regions R1 through R5 by vertical and horizontal splits.
        </shortdescription>
      </image>
    </sidebyside>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Do this repeatedly.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Each time, we can split one of the previously identified regions.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Keep going until some stopping criterion is reached.
            For example, continue until each region has at most 5 observations.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <example>
    <title>Recursive binary splitting with one predictor.</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Consider the following data set with one predictor <m>X</m> and a
        response <m>Y</m>.
        We will use recursive binary splitting to find the best first split for
        the data.
      </p>

      <image source="figures/recursive_splitting_ex1.png" width="90%">
        <shortdescription>A scatter plot of Y versus X.</shortdescription>
      </image>
    </introduction>

    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Sketch all the possible splits for the first split and number them
          from left to right.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <answer>
        <image source="figures/recursive_splitting_ex1_splits.png" width="90%">
          <shortdescription>
            A scatter plot of Y versus X with vertical lines indicating possible
            splits numbered from left to right.
          </shortdescription>
        </image>
      </answer>
    </task>

