        <p>
          The best split is at <m>s = 3.90</m> since it results in the lowest
          total RSS of 3.107.
        </p>

        <p>
          <alert>
    This table was generated with the help of ChatGPT. Please double-check the calculations and ensure that they are correct.
    </alert>
        </p>
      </answer>
    </task>

    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Assume we impose a minimum leaf size of 2 observations.
          The optimal split in this case is at <m>X=3.9</m>.
          What is the predicted response <m>\hat{Y}</m> for a new observation
          with <m>X=3.5</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>

      <answer>
        <p>
          Since <m>X=3.5</m> is less than the split point of <m>3.9</m>, it
          falls into the left region <m>R_1</m>.
          The predicted response <m>\hat{Y}</m> for this new observation is the
          mean response of the training observations in <m>R_1</m>, which is
          <m>3.233</m>.
        </p>
      </answer>
    </task>
  </example>

  <paragraphs>
    <title>Pruning</title>

    <sidebyside widths="45% 45%">
      <image source="figures/Fig8-4.png">
        <shortdescription>
          Complex regression tree for baseball player salaries featuring
          multiple splits on Years, Hits, RBI, Walks, Runs, and Putouts.
        </shortdescription>
      </image>

      <stack>
        <p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                Big trees leave you open to potential overfitting.
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                Could just stop building earlier, but that's short-sighted.
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                Instead, grow a big tree and prune it back.
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                Find the subtree with the best test error rate.
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                There are too many subtrees to test them all.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </stack>
    </sidebyside>
  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs>
    <title>Result of pruning</title>

    <image source="figures/Fig8-5.png" width="75%">
      <shortdescription>
        Plot of Mean Squared Error versus tree size for Training,
        Cross-Validation, and Test sets, including error bars for each.
      </shortdescription>
    </image>

    <sidebyside widths="49% 49%">
      <image source="figures/Fig8-4.png">
        <shortdescription>
          Complex regression tree for baseball player salaries featuring
          multiple splits on Years, Hits, RBI, Walks, Runs, and Putouts.
        </shortdescription>
      </image>

      <image source="figures/Fig8-1.png">
        <shortdescription>
          Pruned regression tree with three terminal nodes.
        </shortdescription>
      </image>
    </sidebyside>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Result of pruning is the three-leaf tree on the right.
            This example technically uses a complex pruning method, but we will
            not discuss its details in this course.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs>
    <title>Decision trees for classification: Basic idea</title>

    <image source="figures/Fig8-6c.png" width="60%">
      <shortdescription>
        Classification tree for heart disease predicting Yes or No based on
        splits of Thal, Ca, MaxHR, and ChestPain.
      </shortdescription>

      <description>
        <p>
          Example decision tree for classificaiton using the <c>Heart</c>
          dataset.
        </p>
      </description>
    </image>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Can't use RSS; need an error rate.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Define <m>\hat p_{mk}</m> = proportion of training observations in
            <m>R_m</m> from the <m>k</m>th class.
            Then the classification error for region <m>R_m</m> is given by:
            <md>
              <mrow>E \amp= 1-\max_k(\hat p_{mk})</mrow>
              <mrow>\amp= \frac{\text{no.~of minority class observations in } R_m}{\text{total observations in } R_m}</mrow>
            </md>
          </p>

          <p>
            <ul>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Fraction of training observations not in the most common class
                  for that region.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>            
            The <term>split error</term> for classification is the weighted
            average of the errors for the left and right nodes, where the
            weights are the proportions of observations in each node.
            The formula for the error <m>E_s</m> is given below for split
            <m>s</m>.
            <md>
              \text{E}_s = \frac{N_{\text{left}}}{N} \times E_{\text{left}} + \frac{N_{\text{right}}}{N} \times E_{\text{right}}
            </md>
            where <m>N</m> is the number of points in the parent node,
            <m>N_{\text{left}}</m> and <m>N_{\text{right}}</m> are the number of
            points in the left and right child nodes, respectively.
            <m>E_{\text{left}}</m> and <m>E_{\text{right}}</m> are the
            classification errors for the left and right child nodes,
            respectively.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            In practice, the splits are decided based on <term>Gini index</term>
            or <term>cross-entropy</term>, but we will not discuss those in our
            class.
            <ul>
              <li>
                <p>
                  We will use the misclassification error rate for by-hand
                  computation of the splits in our class, which is easier to
                  understand and compute.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Gini index, cross-entropy, or misclassification error rate can be
            used for pruning; however, the prediction accuracy is the goal, then
            classification error rate is preferable for pruning.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs>
    <title>Example</title>

    <image source="figures/Fig8-6a.png">
      <shortdescription>
        Large, complex classification tree for heart disease predicting Yes or
        No based on numerous clinical features and splits.
      </shortdescription>
    </image>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            The terminal nodes here: Yes = person has heart disease; No = no
            heart disease.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs>
    <title>Pruning the example</title>

    <sidebyside widths="45% 35%">
      <image source="figures/Fig8-6b.png">
        <shortdescription>
          Plot of classification error versus tree size for Training,
          Cross-Validation, and Test sets, with error bars for each.
        </shortdescription>
      </image>

      <image source="figures/Fig8-6c.png">
        <shortdescription>
          Classification tree for heart disease predicting Yes or No based on
          splits of Thal, Ca, MaxHR, and ChestPain.
        </shortdescription>
      </image>
    </sidebyside>
  </paragraphs>

  <exercise>
    <title>Classification trees.</title>

    <introduction>
      <p/>
      <image source="figures/decision_trees_classification_prediction_ex1.png">
        <shortdescription>
          TWo figures showing two decision trees with different data, but the
          same partitions.
          The one on the right (b) has in addition one test point shown as a
          square.
        </shortdescription>
      </image>
    </introduction>

    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          For the decision tree shown in Figure (a), what is the classification
          error rate for <m>R_1</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          The classification error rate for <m>R_1</m> is <m>0.4</m>.
          This is because there are 5 observations in <m>R_1</m> and 2 of them
          are misclassified.
          Therefore, the error rate is <m>\frac{2}{5} = 0.4</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          For the decision tree shown in Figure (b), determine the predicted
          class for the shown point.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <answer>
        <p>
          The predicted class for the shown point is circle, since that's the
          majority class in the partition that the point falls into.
        </p>
      </answer>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <paragraphs>
    <title>Linear models vs trees</title>

    <sidebyside widths="45% 45%">
      <stack>
        <image source="figures/Fig8-7-top.png">
          <shortdescription>
            Two plots comparing a linear decision boundary with a decision tree
            approximation using axis-aligned rectangular splits in feature
            space.
          </shortdescription>
        </image>

        <p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                Obviously, the linear model does better here.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </stack>

      <stack>
        <image source="figures/Fig8-7-bottom.png">
          <shortdescription>
            Two plots showing a decision tree perfectly capturing a rectangular
            boundary with axis-aligned splits, compared to a linear boundary.
          </shortdescription>
        </image>

        <p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                Not going to beat this case, though.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </stack>
    </sidebyside>
  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs>
    <title>Pros/Cons</title>

    <sidebyside widths="45% 45%">
      <stack>
        <p>
          Pros:
        </p>

        <p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                Trees are very easy to explain to people.
                Often easier to explain than linear regression!
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                Trees can be displayed graphically and are easily interpreted
                even by a non-expert, especially if they are small.
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                Trees can easily handle qualitative predictors without the need
                to create dummy variables.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </stack>

      <stack>
        <p>
          Cons:
        </p>

        <p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                Not as accurate as other methods of classification and
                regression.
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                Not robust: a small change in the data can cause a large change
                in the estimated tree.
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                Fix: aggregate many decision trees.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </stack>
    </sidebyside>
  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs>
    <title>TL;DR</title>

    <sidebyside widths="45% 40%">
      <image source="figures/Fig8-1.png">
        <shortdescription>
          Regression tree for baseball player salaries splitting on Years and
          Hits to produce three leaf nodes.
        </shortdescription>
      </image>

      <image source="figures/Fig8-2.png" width="80%">
        <shortdescription>
          Scatter plot of Hits versus Years partitioned into three regions R1,
          R2, and R3 by a regression tree.
        </shortdescription>
      </image>
    </sidebyside>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Split into regions by greedily decreasing RSS.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Prune the tree using cost complexity.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Not robust: next time, figure out how to aggregate trees.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

